uh  this session uh the first speaker  is  that  um so yeah i'm looking forward to  spending the next two years as a postdoc  here at berkeley um today i want to tell  you about some of the things i've been  thinking about over the last few years  um regarding what algorithic insights  from tcs can tell us about modern  challenges in data science now this  turns out to be a fairly subtle endeavor  because of a fundamental disconnect  between the way we traditionally reason  about algorithm design and the kinds of  problems that arise in data science  applications let's take something like  max flow you know the gold standard  we're taught is that we should have an  algorithm that if you fed it in  arbitrary worst case instance correctly  spits out a max flow after running a  polynomial amount of time and by now we  have a fairly mature understanding of  which problems on this map you can hope  to achieve this gold standard  for instance sat solving or craft  coloring uh sorry um and which questions  for instance that solving your graph  coloring for which is likely to be  impossible  you can draw a similar looking map for  problems in data science  but actually through this traditional  lens of worst case analysis a more  accurate depiction would be something  like this  and the reason is that most  ml problems if posed from this worst  case perspective are just hopelessly  hard  so to get actionable theory for machine  learning it's not enough to just develop  the right algorithmic primitives you  really need the right mathematical  models to go along with them  this begs two questions which have been  the focus of my work first have we  converged to the right toolbox for  dealing with fundamental problems in  data science and two are we looking at  the right models in this talk i want to  tell you about some natural settings  where new techniques are provably  necessary  as well as instances where working with  the right or wrong model can have  consequences far beyond the real world  theory  the course of advancing this viewpoint  my research has touched upon most of the  regions on this map but in the interest  of time i want to hone in on my work in  one particular area namely the design of  algorithms that are robust to a number  of any number of real-world deviations  in your data  okay so to unpack what i mean by  robustness let's talk about some of the  common challenges that arise in working  with modern datasets  so it's a fact of life that modern data  can be extremely high dimensional  it can be very heavy-tailed for instance  if you plot how often different  categories of objects appear in standard  scene recognition benchmarks you'll find  many categories with a very small number  of occurrences  when it comes to things like online  reinforcement learning data can be  generated in a dynamic non-iid fashion  that may even depend on the actions you  choose  and data could be misspecified in the  sense that there's a disconnect between  the setting where you know that your  email system works the spec  and the setting where you want to  actually want to deploy it  so for instance your spec could be some  great theorem you proved about learning  over linearly separable data and reality  could be that the data set in the wild  that you want to apply your algorithm to  simply doesn't satisfy this assumption  spec could be the traditional antenna  supervised learning pipeline and reality  could be that there's an adversary  that's able to tamper with some fraction  of your data for instance in the context  of a data poisoning attack  now my work is focused on designing new  algorithms for basic learning problems  to meet these kinds of challenges and  for the sake of concreteness i want to  tell you about one very recent result of  ours joined with fred kohler ankur  moytra and  that seeks to address all four of these  challenges simultaneously  so let me just tell you sort of one  nugget from this paper  just consider the standard you know  setting of linear regression  but with the following twist let's  imagine that a random but constant  fraction of the data has been  arbitrarily corrupted  okay so just to recall  the standard linear regression setup we  get x y pairs where the y's are noisy  linear functions of the x's  where the linear function is  parametrized by some vector w  let's adopt some standard scalings so  let's say that the x's are you know  normalized have unit norm and w has norm  at most r where you should think of r as  like the dynamic range of the problem  and we can imagine the noise is maybe  coming from a gaussian with variance  sigma squared and here we should think  of sigma as being significantly smaller  than r  okay so this is just standard you know  fixed design and regression  now let's imagine an adversary comes  along and is able to tamper with a  randomly chosen 10 percent of your data  so in particular this adversary you can  imagine as being all powerful it  inspects you know it has access to the  full data set  and can run in as much time as you want  and eventually they're going to  they're going to change these y values  to whatever they want  and we as the learner only observe xy  pairs  okay so what's our objective here our  objective is to compete with the  all-powerful baseline which actually  knew exactly which points were corrupted  because if you knew which points were  corrupted you could of course just throw  them out of the data set and run  ordinarily squares on the rest of the  data  and the objective we want to minimize is  what we'll call the clean mean squared  error  roughly speaking this is the average  squared distance between your prediction  which is w hat inner product x  and the ground truth label without noise  w inner product x  let me emphasize that while there have  been a number of exciting recent works  on robust statistics and high dimensions  the model that i've just described is  sort of a departure from these works in  the sense that we're not making any  kinds of strong assumptions on the  process that actually generates the xis  in particular not only could they you  know be iid from some arbitrary  distribution these xi's could actually  arrive in some adaptive fashion  okay so what do we show  so one result from this paper shows that  for any value of corruption  from zero to a half  we give an efficient algorithm for the  supercontaminated regression problem  that achieves clean mean squared error  which is essentially optimal um up the  law factors  and because our algorithm is able to  tolerate  adaptively chosen covariates  we're actually able to get extensions to  challenging online problems for instance  we get the first algorithm for linear  contextual bandits that's able to handle  a constant fraction of uh the time spent  time steps being corrupted and the  regret that we achieve in this context  is also optimal to log factors  to complement these results we also show  that you can't actually achieve these  kinds of guarantees using off-the-shelf  techniques like grading descent to  minimize the convex loss for instance  minimizing the l1 loss or hebrew loss  and so at this juncture let me tell you  a bit about the algorithm  the nice thing is that the analysis is a  bit tricky but the algorithm itself is  very simple to state  so we're going to minimize this notion  of squared error  over  vectors w hat  and simultaneously over subsets of the  data set so here you should think of the  subset s as our guess for which points  in the data set have been have not been  corrupted  and this s where in the optimization is  going to range over  some family of large subsets of the data  which behave quasi randomly and uh the  technical details aren't um  uh having to talk about offline but the  algorithm you know once we have this in  place is quite simple so we initialize  our guess for which points aren't  corrupted to be all points  and then we just repeat the following  i keep s fixed and i refit w hat  then i keep w hat fixed and i refit s  okay so you can think of this as just  some kind of alternating minimization  procedure  and the point is that after you run this  for enough steps you converge to an  approximate stationary point for this  objective  and the key structural result which is  proof i won't tell you about is that an  approximate stationary point for this  problem suffices to achieve this near  optimal clean mean squared error  guarantee  okay in the remaining time let me tell  you about one experiment in a related  setting um on real world data  so in a different paper with the same  set of authors we explored sort of the  analogous setting for classification so  here the y values  are binary labels instead of real valued  okay and for instance this uh  the model that we considered uh captures  learning half spaces with bisar noise  the algorithm that we consider turns out  on the surface to be  quite similar it's also based on some  kind of iterative re-weighing of the  data steps to mitigate the effect of  corruptions  let me just tell you about the  experiment so we we ran our algorithm on  the uci adult data sets here the task is  given the demographic info for an  individual tell whether they make more  than 50k  and we injected into this data set a  particular attack the details aren't  super important but suffice it to say  that we're targeting a specific  sub-population of the data and so you  can think of it as injecting noise but  in a heterogeneous fashion into the data  set  okay so what happens if you train on  this noisy data  so we compare it against three different  baselines random forest logistic  regression and minimizing a particular  context loss  and as you vary the noise rate on the  x-axis  and look at the resulting you know  classifier that you get out of these  algorithms you'll notice that they do  fairly similarly  if you measure the accuracy over the  entire population  but if you restrict your attention to  for instance uh people in the target  group who do make more than 50k then you  see a significant degradation in the  performance of classifiers obtained by  logistic regression and this context  loss  as the noise rate increases okay  in fact this phenomena is quite robust  across different kinds of subpopulations  and  you know this is just some empirical  observation but it suggests that perhaps  being robust in richer noise models  isn't just some kind of mathematical  exercise it really helps guide the  design of better algorithms downstream  okay so in the meaning time let me just  briefly mention some other things i've  worked on so it turns out this general  blueprint of  iterative re-weighing schemes has also  been useful in my work on secure  distributed learning and even in more  traditional learning theory settings  where you actually assume the data might  come from some nice distribution like a  gaussian and you're trying to learn some  complex function class like neural  networks  here the idea of carefully re-weighing  the data turns out to be useful even in  these non-robust settings for instance  yielding the first example of a neural  network class that you can learn  efficiently but provably not by noisy  grading scent on a student network for  instance  i've also worked on mixture models this  is like a stylized model for  heterogeneous data  so i've worked on a sort of  heterogeneous variance of robust linear  aggression  a latent variable model called mixtures  of product distributions  and turns out related ideas for learning  mixture models have also been useful for  settling some old debates in the natural  sciences so in classical optics for  instance by rephrasing the problem with  optical resolution as a mixture model  learning problem we were actually able  to refute the long-standing belief that  half the wavelength of light marks the  point at which optical resolution  becomes impossible for classical systems  among other inverse problems in the  sciences i've also worked on quantum  learning where even the sample  complexity for various basic learning  tasks remain open  here it turns out that tools from  showing lower bounds for online decision  making actually are perfect fit for  showing separations for learning with  and without quantum memory for a variety  of tasks like shadow tomography state  certification etc  and more broadly you know i'm interested  in tcs as a whole and i've spent some  time also thinking about algorithms for  counting and sampling  um you know here are some questions that  i'm very excited to think about over the  next two years if any of these piqued  your interest please do reach out it  would be great to collaborate  um why do you think random firsts do so  well  yeah yeah i always get this good yeah so  um  so for a bit of context i guess the one  baseline  like theory baseline that we're trying  to sort of um  improve on was this improper learner of  um  dear clinical us at all um and you know  even though the algorithm is quite  different from random force you can kind  of think of both as trying to find some  partition of space and sort of uh  classifiers that do well over these  different parts of the partition  so in some sense we're trying to get  some kind of simple classifier that is  able to match this baseline and it  stands to reason that maybe like  algorithms that partition up the space  in different ways might be able to  mitigate this  this kind of  violation of quality of opportunities  great questions  [Applause]

